.TH ZIPOPT 1 "October 2026" "Xtro-ZIP" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zipopt \- losslessly optimize ZIP archive members by trying available compressors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zipopt
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-methods= LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-zopfli-iterations= N ]
.RB [ \-\-dcl-implode-optimal ]
.I input.zip output.zip
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zipopt
reads and verifies a single-disk ZIP archive, extracts each member internally,
tries all selected compressor configurations, and writes the smallest eligible
compressed representation into a new archive. It retains an original compressed
stream only when its ZIP method is permitted by the selected profile.
The entire completed output is re-extracted and compared byte-for-byte against
the source before it is published. No external Zip or UnZip command is launched.
Output files are never overwritten. Each member name is displayed before
optimization; directory entries are written as empty STORE records without
trying every compressor. Each member begins with a right-aligned one-based
progress count out of all central-directory members (including directories),
followed by its original zero-based index. The selected-result line reports
uncompressed member size, original packed member size, and selected packed
member size. A second, aligned line gives three rounded ZIP-style percentages:
original compression relative to uncompressed size, improvement relative to
original packed size, and final compression relative to uncompressed size,
followed by the number of packed bytes saved or added. Verbose mode lists the original
packed stream and each attempted candidate in descending packed-size order,
largest first; ties place the original first, then candidates in registry
order. Each verbose row reports the packed size, the percentage compressed
relative to the uncompressed member size, and the signed percentage change
relative to the original packed stream. A plus sign means larger, a minus
sign means smaller, and an equals sign means unchanged. Changes from zero
packed bytes to nonzero bytes report n/a. The original row identifies its
actual ZIP method. Columns are right-aligned to the widest observed values
for each member, and member records are separated with a blank line.
On successful publication, the output filename prefixes the completion line
with the total number of members. The next line begins with
.B zipopt:
and reports the sum of member uncompressed sizes and physical original and
output ZIP file sizes (including headers and metadata). A separate line,
indented eight spaces, displays three rounded ratios: initial physical
compression, reduction in physical archive size, and final physical
compression, followed by the number of physical bytes saved or added.
All byte counts have comma-separated thousands (independent of locale), while
member ordinals and counts are not grouped. Verbose mode additionally prints
a method-count table after the completion line and before the archive-size
summary. The table counts methods actually recorded in the output, including
STORE directory entries, and sorts by ascending member count, breaking ties
by method number. Configurations sharing a ZIP method ID are aggregated;
normal mode omits the table. In verbose mode only, the completion header is
separated from the final member report by a blank line.
For multiple selected candidates, flushed dots show progress; one-candidate
runs omit dots. POSIX SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT interrupt
handling removes the named temporary output without publishing it.
.PP
The default
.B \-\-methods=all
tries 19 configurations, which can take considerable time and temporary disk
space. Compression runs sequentially and any failure aborts the operation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v , " \-\-verbose"
Print the original packed stream and each attempted compressor, sorted by
compressed size in descending order.
.TP
.BI \-\-methods= LIST
Comma-separated selection: store, shrink, reduce-l1, reduce-l2, reduce-l3,
reduce-l4, implode-4k-2, implode-4k-3, implode-8k-2, implode-8k-3,
deflate, deflate64, dcl-implode-binary, dcl-implode-ascii, bzip2, lzma,
zstd, xz, ppmd. Use
.B implode
to select all four ZIP Implode variants and
.B dcl-implode
for both DCL modes. Use
.B \-\-methods=help
to display every available method; invalid names also print this list.
.TP
.BI \-\-zopfli-iterations= N
Set Zopfli iteration count for Deflate -11 (1..10000, default 15).
.TP
.B \-\-dcl-implode-optimal
Enable the extremely slow optimal parser for both DCL Implode candidates.
Disabled by default.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Display usage.
.SH INPUT RESTRICTIONS
The input is rejected if encrypted (ZipCrypto, AES, or strong encryption),
signed, spanned/split, contains PKAV or RefPtr, has an SFX prefix, or contains
unrecognized unsafe extra fields. Valid FWKCS MD5 fields are verified and
removed; output never carries FWKCS fields. Plain LZFSE and CMPSC streams can
be decoded but are not generated by the default output profile.
.SH PLATFORM
Version 1 builds on Unix with the bundled codecs, libbz2, liblzma, and libzstd.
Native Windows and DOS execution is not yet supported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zip (1),
.BR unzip (1),
.BR zipdedup (1)
